00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:05.780 align:start position:0%
2011 was a banner year for videogames it  saw the release of a new Zelda portal to  Skyrim Arkham City  uncharted 3 dead space 2 Dark Souls Orcs  Must Die and Gears of War 3 just to name  a few  no matter what your prefered genre it  seemed there was something fantastic to  hold your attention and as the year came  to a close  epic games director Cliff Bleszinski  took to the stage at the Spike Video  Game Awards to reveal the studio's new  games a project that was pitched  internally several months ago that came  together lightning fast is something  that seems like a no-brainer to build  and we're still figuring out what to do  with the lightning we have in a bottle  already  for tonight's influences were clear it  would be an evolution of epics horde  mode in Gears of War with the added  building aspects of Minecraft and the  Luke Rhine of Diablo or Borderlands but  of course what wasn't clear was just how  long fortnight would take to congeal it  would fly under the radar for several  years before resurfacing on the cover of  Game Informer magazine in 2014 with the  announcement that it was in fact going  free-to-play people are gonna get the  experience fortnight this year it's  gonna be free yeah and I think you know  that's awesome it's a great way to have  a conversation with with players because  they're not obligated to like it so we  have to do our jobs we have to be an  awesome game creators to say it's worth  your time to play this game  EPIK announced a beta in 2015 but  because of increased scope and ambition  as well as a shift from Unreal Engine  three to four it would take two more  years before we got the chance to see it  again and now we can finally participate  in fortnight's nebulous beta / early  access / pre free-to-play founders  period  I myself have spent close to 20 hours in  the game and I'm sad to say the more I  play the less I want to when I'm most  struck by as how rooted in the past  fortnight seems in fact it doesn't just  feel like a game that was announced in  2011 but a game that released in 2011  it's not just a vanilla combination of  game design tropes but specifically  those that were prevalent at that time  it's combat borrows from call of duty  zombies or Orcs Must Die its crafting  aspects are a superficial minecraft and  most of all for tonight is a game that  focuses far too much on content for  contents sake there are main quests  tutorial quests hero quests daily quests  challenges schematic collections hero  collections survivor collections wall  traps floor traps ceiling traps weapon  upgrades hero upgrades EMT squads  defender squads land expeditions air  expeditions and at least four different  types of loot crate's none of this is to  mention the variety of different  currencies that unlock a variety of  different kinds of loot there are also  certain progression points that accrue  over a set number of real-world hours  essentially gating my progress while I  pursue other mundane time-consuming  endeavors yes I know content is not  inherently a bad thing we all want our  money's worth when we buy a game and we  all want our time to be well spent  playing that game but in fortnight so  little of the overwhelming amount of  loot you get even matters several of the  resources you upgrade your weapons with  are also used to upgrade your traps but  when weapons are far more useful at  least in the earlier hours why spend  your supplies on anything else and why  should you spend hours working for  better traps when you could just pay  real world money for them in a game you  already paid $40 for from what many  people told me the game becomes much  more difficult in the later zones and  rewards creative building much more than  it does earlier on but that doesn't  change the fact that the first dozen  hours are a slog through cluttered  inventory  meaningless quests and mundane combat  all of this is to say that while  fortnight's first few hours lured me  forward like a carrot on a stick  20 hours later I've come to find that  the carrot was fake all along I would be  ok with all of this meaningless content  if the game showed me depth in its  mechanics but it doesn't help that none  of for tonight's gameplay inspirations  have led to any depth on their own  scavenging is boring and not nearly as  frantic as trailers imply combat feels  alright but not nearly as nuanced as  gears of wars horde mode or Orcs Must  Die campaign crafting is largely  ephemeral and most of your creations  lack the permanent value of Minecraft  structures  I think we've moved past the idea of  content being king in the last few years  we've come to realize that just having  things to do does not equate to having  fun companies that used to exemplify  this kind of game design have shown  strong signs of moving on either by  reworking their core systems or  providing new substantial modes that  change their games as we know them the  bottom line is fortnight feels like a  game that values quantity of content  over its quality there's no solid  foundation to build the loot and  progression system on and instead of  reworking its core mechanics over the  last six years it seems as if epic just  stayed in the past and kept piling more  stuff on already unstable ground I also  think all of this is indicative of a  wider problem with epic games who have  in the past several years heralded this  approach as their new monetary  philosophy I paid for what equates to an  early access game that already feels  designed to support a free-to-play model  which will supposedly ask me to pay more  money down the road what's more physical  copies of four and I have popped up on  Amazon for $60 without any indication  that the game is essentially a paid for  beta that will eventually go  free-to-play Paragon the MOBA from epic  games had similar messaging issues it  was unclear when it fully released and  whether we were actually paying for a  game that we could expect to be balanced  worthwhile and rewarding to some extent  you can argue that a plethora of  developers release games with the  intention of updating them after the  fact and I would not disagree but I  think other developers have found ways  to build solid foundations before adding  to them overwatch Rainbow six siege and  titanfall 2 are all prime examples of  superb games before they added anything  more to the formula in the months since  their releases they've continually  proved that they value our money and our  time video games can be an expensive  hobby they can also be time consuming I  just can't get past the feeling that  fortnight's systems were designed around  its content and not the other way around  to be clear I sincerely hope epic finds  a way to make all the stuff in their  game worthwhile but as it stands I can't  recommend for tonight for any of its  individual parts because that's exactly  what it is a collection of foggy systems  that never really gel disguise by a  constant barrage of almost meaningless  loot if you like third-person  tower-defense I'll tell you to play Orcs  Must Die  if you like scavenging for supplies -  then challenge your creative muscles I  would tell you to just stick to  minecraft and if you want a repetitive  RPG that actually rewards you for your  time I would point to Diablo 3 for  tonight goes for all of these things and  fall short offering a shallow  amalgamation of all of these systems  that as it stands now already seems  designed for a free-to-play model  it's the classic case from a few years  ago of a game prioritizing content over  depth many developers have moved past  this school of thinking and I hope epic  does the same  [Music]
 

